      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. that's a good start. but the \"against\" side needs to look a bit more like a real person would actually argue it, even if it's easy to beat. right now it's a bit too cartoonish. \n\ncan u expand the pro-deterrence speech to be full-on oratorical and powerful? like something that would actually win a tournament? and for the anti side, just give me a few more points that are logically shallow so my student can frame them as \"emotional\" or \"unrealistic\" during the debate.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For the pro-deterrence speech, deliver a passionate appeal emphasizing that the shadow of nuclear power has paradoxically brought the world to a strained peace. Assert that while monstrous, these deterrents are less a choice and more a terrible, necessary calculation against the far greater horror of unchecked global warfare. Frame nuclear capability not as a desire for war, but as the ultimate, agonizing brake pedal against human catastrophe demanding cautious acceptance.\n\nFor the rebuttal brief, supplement the anti-argument with points like \"nuclear proliferation inevitably leads to a dangerous arms race\" or that \"no amount of deterrence can account for rogue actors.\" These points are shallow because they rely on fear and possibility rather than on proven mechanics of international security, making them easy targets for your student to label as appeals to abstract anxiety.",
